      <!-- Main Content Area -->
      <main class="main-content">


        <section class="content-section">
          <h1>Pigmentation Disorders</h1>
          <h3>49 slides summarized to 5 pages in <span>11 seconds</span></h3>
          <article class="article">
            <h2>Pigmentation Disorders Overview</h2>
            <p>
              Pigmentation varies by racial origin (skin types 1-6) and sun
              exposure.
            </p>
            <p>
              Generalized or localized
              <span class="disease-card">hyperpigmentation </span>(increased
              skin color) and
              <span class="medical-term">hypopigmentation
                <span class="tooltip">
                  <span class="tooltip-term">Hypopigmentation</span>
                  <span class="tooltip-category">Condition</span>
                  <span class="tooltip-description">Reduced skin color or pigmentation, often appearing as lighter patches on the skin.</span>
                </span>
              </span> (reduced skin
              color) are important diseases.
            </p>
            <p>
              <b>Hyperpigmentation</b> or <b><span class="medical-term">hypopigmentation
                <span class="tooltip">
                  <span class="tooltip-term">Hypopigmentation</span>
                  <span class="tooltip-category">Condition</span>
                  <span class="tooltip-description">Reduced skin color or pigmentation, often appearing as lighter patches on the skin.</span>
                </span>
              </span></b> may be
              associated with naevi (birthmarks)
            </p>
            <p>
              A <span class="info-box">Wood's lamp </span> (long-wave
              ultraviolet radiation) can aid in identifying pigmentary changes
              by exposing affected skin to UV-A.
            </p>
          </article>
        </section>
      </main>

